A = {
"sa-pokhran1998": dict(
 bluf="India's and Pakistan's nuclear tests made South Asia openly nuclear-armed, turning their rivalry into one of the world's most dangerous. Mutual deterrence has since coexisted with repeated crises, from the Kargil war of 1999 to the strikes of 2019 and 2025.",
 lens="Nuclear weapons deter all-out war but can embolden limited conflicts below that threshold."),
"la199951": dict(
 bluf="Hugo Chávez's election began a 'Bolivarian revolution' that spent oil wealth on social programmes while concentrating power, and it helped launch Latin America's 'pink tide' of leftist governments. Oil dependence and the erosion of institutions set up Venezuela's later collapse.",
 lens="Petro-states that spend oil windfalls without building institutions are exposed when prices fall."),
"la200152": dict(
 bluf="Argentina's 2001 default, then the largest sovereign default in history, ended a decade of pegging the peso to the dollar and plunged half the population into poverty. It is a defining case of how a fixed exchange rate and foreign debt can collapse together.",
 now="Argentina has defaulted nine times in its history, and the 2001 crisis still shapes its politics, from the Kirchners to Milei.",
 lens="A currency peg buys credibility until the economy can no longer bear it, and then breaks all at once."),
"na200190": dict(
 bluf="Al-Qaeda's attacks on New York and Washington killed nearly 3,000 people, the deadliest terrorist attack in history. They launched the US 'War on Terror' (wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, mass surveillance and a global campaign against jihadist groups), which defined two decades of world politics.",
 why="Al-Qaeda opposed the US military presence in Saudi Arabia and US support for Israel and for Arab governments it considered apostate, and hoped to provoke a war that would rally Muslims to its cause.",
 trig="Nineteen hijackers seized four airliners on the morning of September 11, 2001.",
 then="The United States invaded Afghanistan (2001) and Iraq (2003), created the Department of Homeland Security, expanded surveillance and held detainees at Guantánamo. The wars cost trillions of dollars and hundreds of thousands of lives.",
 lens="Terrorism aims to provoke overreaction: how a state responds can matter more than the attack itself."),
"as200173b": dict(
 bluf="The US-led invasion toppled the Taliban within weeks, but the 20-year effort to build a stable Afghan state failed. The Taliban's return in 2021 made it a lesson in the limits of nation-building by outside powers.",
 lens="Defeating a regime is quick; building a legitimate state in its place is slow, and may be impossible from outside."),
"as200173": dict(
 bluf="China's entry into the World Trade Organization tied the world's most populous country into global trade and made it the 'workshop of the world'. It raised Chinese living standards and lowered prices worldwide, but hit manufacturing towns in the West, the 'China shock' that feeds today's trade wars.",
 why="Deng's heirs saw global markets as the path to growth, and Western governments hoped trade would encourage political liberalization in China.",
 now="Trade tensions grew into US-China tariff wars from 2018, and the bet that trade would liberalize China's politics is widely judged to have failed.",
 lens="Economic integration with a rival creates mutual dependence, and mutual vulnerability."),
"af200255": dict(
 bluf="The African Union replaced the OAU with a body that claimed the right to intervene in member states to stop genocide and war crimes, a break with strict non-interference. Its peacekeeping and mediation have had mixed results.",
 lens="Institutions rewrite their rules after failures: Rwanda pushed Africa from non-interference toward 'non-indifference'."),
"eu2002114": dict(
 bluf="The euro gave twelve countries one currency and one monetary policy run by the European Central Bank. It eased trade and symbolized unity, but locked very different economies together without a shared budget, a flaw exposed by the debt crisis of 2010.",
 now="About 350 million people use the euro, in 21 countries since Bulgaria joined in 2026.",
 lens="A monetary union without a fiscal union pushes the stress onto its weakest members."),
"se-timor": dict(
 bluf="East Timor's independence, after a UN-supervised vote and violent Indonesian reprisals, ended 24 years of occupation in which an estimated 100,000 to 180,000 people died. It was the 21st century's first new state and a rare case of the UN guiding a country to statehood.",
 lens="Democratization in an occupying power can open the door to independence for the territories it holds."),
"af200356": dict(
 bluf="In Darfur, Sudan's government and its Janjaweed militias killed hundreds of thousands, and the International Criminal Court issued its first arrest warrant for a sitting head of state. The same militias later became the RSF, a central force in Sudan's current war.",
 lens="Militias armed by a state to fight its wars can become rivals to that state."),
"as200374": dict(
 bluf="The US-led invasion toppled Saddam Hussein but rested on claims about weapons of mass destruction that proved false. The occupation's failures led to insurgency, sectarian war and the rise of ISIS, increased Iran's influence and damaged US credibility worldwide.",
 lens="Removing a regime without a plan for what follows can unleash forces worse than the regime itself."),
"la200353": dict(
 bluf="Lula's election brought a former union leader to power in Brazil, combining social programmes such as Bolsa Família with market-friendly policies. Tens of millions rose out of poverty, though corruption scandals later engulfed his party.",
 lens="Pairing redistribution with economic stability can broaden a centre-left coalition."),
"eu2004115": dict(
 bluf="The EU's eastern enlargement anchored ten countries, mostly former Soviet-bloc states, in the West's political and economic system. It boosted their growth and democracy, set off large migration westward, and was seen by Russia as encroachment.",
 lens="Membership in a rules-based club can lock in democratic and market reforms."),
"se-tsunami": dict(
 bluf="The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami killed about 230,000 people in 14 countries, one of the deadliest natural disasters in history. It led to an Indian Ocean warning system and, in Aceh, helped end a decades-old insurgency.",
 lens="Disasters can open the way to peace by changing the calculations of warring parties."),
"eu2008116": dict(
 bluf="Russia's five-day war with Georgia halted Georgia's NATO ambitions and established Russian control over two breakaway regions. The West's mild response is widely seen as having encouraged Russia's later moves in Ukraine.",
 lens="Mild responses to a first use of force can encourage the next."),
"oc-apology": dict(
 bluf="Australia's formal apology to the Stolen Generations acknowledged a policy of removing Aboriginal children from their families. It was a milestone of reconciliation, though gaps in health, education and incarceration remain wide.",
 lens="Apologies matter symbolically; outcomes depend on what follows."),
"d-lehman": dict(
 bluf="Lehman Brothers' bankruptcy turned a housing slump into a global financial panic. It forced governments to rescue banks and showed that some financial institutions were too interconnected to fail without bringing down the system.",
 lens="Letting one big institution fail can trigger a chain reaction; the dilemma is moral hazard versus systemic risk."),
"na200892": dict(
 bluf="The 2008 financial crisis, triggered by a US housing bubble and risky mortgage securities, caused the worst global recession since the 1930s. Bank bailouts while millions lost homes and jobs fuelled anger that drove populist movements on both left and right.",
 why="Subprime lending, complex securities built on it, heavy borrowing by banks and too little regulation, fed by global savings flows and low interest rates.",
 trig="The bankruptcy of Lehman Brothers in September 2008.",
 then="The Great Recession cost millions of jobs and homes. Governments bailed out banks, central banks cut rates to zero and bought bonds, and new rules forced banks to hold more capital.",
 lens="Financial crises that seem to reward insiders and punish ordinary people produce political backlash for a decade or more."),
"na200892b": dict(
 bluf="Barack Obama's election as the first Black US president was a historic milestone, carried by the financial crisis and opposition to the Iraq War. His presidency passed major health and financial reforms but also saw deepening political polarization.",
 lens="Historic firsts can coincide with, and provoke, intensified backlash."),
"eu2010117": dict(
 bluf="The eurozone debt crisis exposed the flaw of a single currency without shared budgets: Greece and others faced bankruptcy and harsh austerity in return for bailouts. It nearly broke the euro and fuelled anti-EU populism across Europe.",
 lens="In a currency union, the weakest members bear the adjustment, politically as well as economically."),
"la201054": dict(
 bluf="The 2010 earthquake killed well over 100,000 people, by some estimates more than 200,000, in the poorest country in the Americas. Despite billions in pledged aid, reconstruction largely failed, and UN peacekeepers introduced cholera.",
 lens="Disasters kill more where states are weak, and aid without strong local institutions often fails."),
"na201093": dict(
 bluf="The Affordable Care Act was the biggest expansion of US health coverage since the 1960s, reaching about 20 million more people. It became a defining partisan battle and survived repeated repeal attempts and court challenges.",
 lens="Social programmes become harder to repeal once millions depend on them."),
"na201194": dict(
 bluf="The killing of Osama bin Laden removed al-Qaeda's founder and symbol ten years after 9/11. That he had lived for years near Pakistan's main military academy strained US-Pakistan relations.",
 lens="Killing a terrorist leader can weaken a group but rarely ends the movement."),
"as201175": dict(
 bluf="The Arab Spring was a wave of protests that toppled long-ruling dictators in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen. Apart from Tunisia, at least for a decade, it ended in restored authoritarianism or civil war, but it showed how deep the grievances against Arab regimes ran.",
 trig="The self-immolation of Tunisian street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi in December 2010.",
 then="Only Tunisia became a democracy; Egypt returned to military rule, and Libya, Syria and Yemen fell into civil wars that drew in outside powers, produced refugee flows to Europe and allowed ISIS to rise.",
 lens="Toppling a dictator is easier than building what replaces him; the most organized forces, often the army or Islamists, win the aftermath."),
"d-mubarak": dict(
 bluf="Mubarak's fall after 18 days of mass protest showed the power of a popular uprising, and the army's role as kingmaker. Egypt's brief democratic experiment ended in a military coup in 2013.",
 lens="When the army decides a leader must go, he goes, and the army often stays."),
"as201175b": dict(
 bluf="Syria's civil war, which began with the regime's crackdown on protests, killed about half a million people and displaced half the population. It drew in Russia, Iran, Turkey, the United States and others, created the conditions for ISIS and sent millions of refugees abroad; Assad finally fell in December 2024.",
 lens="Regimes that choose force over reform can survive for years but destroy their country in the process."),
"as201175c": dict(
 bluf="The Fukushima meltdowns, caused by an earthquake and tsunami, were the worst nuclear accident since Chernobyl. They led Germany to phase out nuclear power and Japan to shut its reactors for years, shaping the global debate on nuclear energy and climate.",
 lens="Rare disasters can reshape energy policy far beyond the country where they happen."),
"af201157": dict(
 bluf="South Sudan's independence ended decades of war with Khartoum, but within two years a civil war broke out between its own leaders. It shows how independence won by armed struggle can leave a state without working institutions.",
 lens="Liberation movements often struggle to become governments."),
"d-libya2011": dict(
 bluf="NATO's intervention helped rebels overthrow Muammar Gaddafi, but Libya then fragmented into rival governments and militias. It made Russia and China wary of future UN-authorized interventions, and turned Libya into a hub for migration and arms.",
 lens="An intervention to protect civilians can become regime change, and regime change without a plan leaves chaos."),
"as201276": dict(
 bluf="Xi Jinping became China's most powerful leader since Mao, centralizing control, cracking down on corruption and dissent, and scrapping presidential term limits. His more assertive foreign policy and tighter control at home have defined the US-China rivalry.",
 lens="Concentrating power can make a state more decisive, and also more prone to costly mistakes."),
"af201458": dict(
 bluf="The 2014-16 Ebola epidemic in West Africa killed over 11,000 people and exposed weak health systems and a slow global response. It led to faster international outbreak responses and an Ebola vaccine.",
 lens="Epidemics expose state weakness: public health is national security."),
"as201477": dict(
 bluf="ISIS's self-declared 'caliphate' held territory across Iraq and Syria, committed genocide against the Yazidis and inspired attacks worldwide. Destroying it took a US-led coalition and local forces until 2019, and its offshoots remain active in Africa and Asia.",
 lens="Jihadist movements thrive in the vacuum left by state collapse and sectarian exclusion."),
"eu2014118": dict(
 bluf="Russia's annexation of Crimea was the first forcible annexation of territory in Europe since World War II. The West's limited sanctions and Russia's backing of separatists in the Donbas set the stage for the full-scale invasion of 2022.",
 lens="Faits accomplis that go unreversed encourage more of them."),
"la201455": dict(
 bluf="Venezuela's economic collapse, driven by oil dependence, mismanagement and corruption and deepened by sanctions, shrank the economy by about three-quarters. Nearly 8 million people, about a quarter of the population, have left, one of the world's largest displacement crises.",
 lens="Petro-states without diversified economies collapse when oil revenue falls."),
"d-euromaidan": dict(
 bluf="The Euromaidan revolution drove out Ukraine's pro-Russian president after months of protest against corruption and the turn away from Europe. Russia responded by seizing Crimea and backing separatists, starting the war that escalated in 2022.",
 lens="A neighbour's revolution can look like an existential threat to a regime that fears the same at home."),
"d-mh17": dict(
 bluf="The downing of MH17 by a Russian-made missile killed all 298 aboard and brought the war in eastern Ukraine to the world's attention. It hardened Western sanctions, and a Dutch court later established the role of Russian-backed forces.",
 lens="Proxy wars draw in third countries when weapons are used carelessly."),
"eu2015119": dict(
 bluf="The 2015 refugee crisis brought over a million people to Europe in one year and overwhelmed EU asylum rules. It transformed European politics: anti-immigration parties rose, and the EU shifted to tighter borders and deals with transit countries such as Turkey.",
 lens="Large, sudden migrations reshape the politics of receiving countries far more than gradual ones."),
"me-jcpoa": dict(
 bluf="The Iran nuclear deal limited Iran's nuclear programme in exchange for sanctions relief, a rare diplomatic success. The US withdrawal in 2018 unravelled it, Iran expanded enrichment, and in 2025 Israel and the United States struck Iran's nuclear sites.",
 lens="Agreements that depend on one government's continued support can collapse with a change of leader."),
"eu2016120": dict(
 bluf="Britain's vote to leave the EU was the first time a member chose to exit, driven by concerns over sovereignty and immigration. It reshaped British politics, strained the union with Scotland and Northern Ireland, and became a symbol of the populist backlash against globalization.",
 why="Concerns over sovereignty and immigration, long-standing British ambivalence about Europe, and discontent in regions that felt left behind.",
 lens="Referendums on complex questions can expose social divides that elections had hidden."),
"la201656": dict(
 bluf="The peace deal with the FARC ended Latin America's longest-running insurgency after 52 years. Narrowly rejected in a referendum, it was revised and approved by Congress, and its implementation has been uneven as other armed groups expanded.",
 lens="Peace agreements end wars between the signatories, not violence by those left out."),
"na201695": dict(
 bluf="Donald Trump's election as an outsider promising 'America First' reflected anger over trade, immigration and the political establishment. It moved US policy toward tariffs and scepticism of alliances and international institutions, and reshaped the Republican Party.",
 why="Discontent over trade and lost manufacturing jobs, immigration, and distrust of the political establishment, concentrated in swing states.",
 lens="Voters who feel left behind by globalization can upend established parties."),
"d-wuhan": dict(
 bluf="The identification of a new coronavirus in Wuhan began the COVID-19 pandemic. Early delays in disclosure and the unresolved question of the virus's origin became flashpoints in US-China relations.",
 lens="Transparency in the first weeks of an outbreak can decide whether it stays local or goes global."),
"af202059": dict(
 bluf="Ethiopia's war in Tigray killed hundreds of thousands, many through famine, and involved Eritrean troops and mass atrocities. It shattered Ethiopia's image as a rising African power and exposed the fragility of its ethnic federal system.",
 why="A power struggle between Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed and the Tigray People's Liberation Front, which had dominated Ethiopia until 2018.",
 lens="Ethnic federations can break into war when the centre and a powerful region fight over control."),
"eu2020121": dict(
 bluf="Brexit took effect as Britain left the EU's single market and customs union, creating new trade barriers and a complex arrangement for Northern Ireland. The UK and EU are still working out their relationship.",
 lens="Leaving a deep economic union is slower and costlier than joining it."),
"na202096": dict(
 bluf="The COVID-19 pandemic killed millions worldwide, shut down economies and transformed work, schooling and public health. Vaccines developed with new mRNA technology saved millions of lives, while lockdowns, mandates and misinformation deepened political divisions.",
 why="A new coronavirus, first identified in Wuhan in late 2019, spread rapidly through global travel networks before most countries reacted.",
 then="Vaccines were developed in under a year. Governments spent trillions on relief, and inflation followed in 2021-23.",
 lens="Pandemics test state capacity and public trust; countries with more of both fared better."),
"me-abraham": dict(
 bluf="The Abraham Accords normalized relations between Israel and several Arab states without a Palestinian state, driven by shared concern about Iran and economic interests. They survived the Gaza war, but showed the Palestinian issue could be sidelined, not erased.",
 lens="Shared threats can bring former enemies together faster than resolving their core disputes."),
"d-vaccine": dict(
 bluf="The first COVID-19 vaccines, developed in under a year with mRNA technology, sharply reduced deaths. Their unequal distribution between rich and poor countries exposed global inequities.",
 lens="Long-term public investment in basic science pays off in emergencies."),
"af202160": dict(
 bluf="A wave of military coups in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger expelled French forces and brought in Russian mercenaries, reshaping the Sahel's alliances. The juntas promised security, but jihadist violence has kept growing.",
 lens="Military regimes that blame foreign partners for security failures often fail to do better."),
"as202178": dict(
 bluf="The Taliban's swift capture of Kabul as US forces withdrew ended America's longest war. The Afghan army's collapse showed how dependent it had been on US support, and the Taliban restored strict rule, barring girls from education beyond primary school.",
 lens="Armies built by outsiders often collapse when the outsiders leave."),
"la202157": dict(
 bluf="The assassination of President Jovenel Moïse deepened Haiti's political collapse and left a vacuum that armed gangs filled. Gangs now control most of the capital, and an international security mission has struggled to restore order.",
 lens="When a weak state loses its leader, armed groups fill the vacuum."),
"na202197": dict(
 bluf="The January 6 attack on the Capitol was an attempt by Trump supporters to stop Congress from certifying the 2020 election result. It failed, but it became a defining event in American debates over the peaceful transfer of power and political violence.",
 lens="Democracies depend on losers accepting results; when leaders reject them, institutions are tested."),
"se-myanmar2021": dict(
 bluf="Myanmar's military coup ended a decade of partial democracy and set off a nationwide civil war. Resistance forces and ethnic armies have taken large parts of the country, the most serious challenge the army has ever faced.",
 lens="Coups against popular elected governments can provoke resistance that unites former rivals."),
"oc-aukus": dict(
 bluf="AUKUS commits the United States and Britain to help Australia acquire nuclear-powered submarines, a major step in balancing China's military rise. It tied Australia's defence even more closely to the United States and angered France and China.",
 lens="A rising power prompts its neighbours to deepen alliances with distant great powers."),
"eu2022122": dict(
 bluf="Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine began the largest war in Europe since 1945. It ended the post-Cold War European order, pushed Finland and Sweden into NATO, triggered European rearmament and a shift away from Russian energy, and drew Russia closer to China, Iran and North Korea.",
 why="Putin rejects Ukraine's independent, Western-oriented path, claims Russians and Ukrainians are 'one people', and portrays NATO's eastward expansion as a threat.",
 trig="The invasion of February 24, 2022, after months of military build-up and Russian demands that NATO pledge never to admit Ukraine.",
 then="Ukraine repelled the assault on Kyiv, and the war became a grinding conflict of drones, artillery and trenches. Hundreds of thousands have been killed or wounded, and Finland and Sweden joined NATO.",
 now="It reshaped European security, energy and defence spending; its outcome will shape the credibility of borders and the future of US alliances.",
 lens="Wars launched in expectation of a quick victory often become long wars of attrition."),
"af202361": dict(
 bluf="Sudan's war between the army and the Rapid Support Forces has created the world's largest displacement crisis and spread famine. It is a struggle between two military factions that had jointly overthrown a civilian transition, with outside powers backing each side.",
 lens="When two armed factions share power after a coup, their rivalry can tear the state apart."),
"la202358": dict(
 bluf="Javier Milei's election brought a libertarian 'chainsaw' programme to Argentina, cutting spending sharply to end chronic inflation. It is a test of whether radical austerity can break Argentina's cycle of crises while keeping public support.",
 lens="Chronic economic crises make voters willing to back radical outsiders."),
"d-wagner": dict(
 bluf="The Wagner mutiny, a march on Moscow by Russia's largest mercenary army, was the most serious challenge to Putin's rule in two decades. Its collapse, and Prigozhin's death in a plane crash two months later, showed both the regime's fragility and its ruthlessness.",
 lens="Regimes that outsource violence to private armies risk those armies turning on them."),
"as202379": dict(
 bluf="Hamas's October 7 attack killed about 1,200 people in Israel and took about 250 hostages, the deadliest day for Jews since the Holocaust. It triggered the war in Gaza, drew in Hezbollah, the Houthis and Iran, and transformed Israeli politics and the region's alignments.",
 lens="Surprise attacks exploit complacency; intelligence failures usually come from wrong assumptions, not missing data."),
"as202379b": dict(
 bluf="Israel's war in Gaza after October 7 killed tens of thousands of Palestinians, according to Gaza's health ministry, devastated the territory and caused a humanitarian catastrophe. It widened into conflict with Hezbollah, the Houthis and Iran and led to cases at international courts; a US-brokered ceasefire in October 2025 freed the last living hostages.",
 lens="Urban wars against militants embedded among civilians produce massive civilian tolls and lasting political consequences."),
"oc-falepili": dict(
 bluf="The Falepili Union gives Tuvaluans a pathway to live in Australia as rising seas threaten their islands, alongside Australian security guarantees. It is the first treaty to create a migration route specifically because of climate change.",
 lens="Climate change is becoming a question of security and migration, not only of the environment."),
"na202499": dict(
 bluf="Donald Trump's return to the presidency, sweeping all seven swing states, confirmed populism's hold on American politics. His second term has brought sweeping tariffs, expansive use of executive power and a reshaping of US alliances and trade.",
 lens="Political movements that survive defeat can return stronger and reshape a party permanently."),
}
